<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><ncx xmlns="http://www.daisy.org/z3986/2005/ncx/" version="2005-1" xml:lang="en"><head><meta name="dtb:uid" content="urn:uuid:40870566-f652-463c-a10d-6406394f40b9"/><meta name="dtb:depth" content="2"/><meta name="dtb:totalPageCount" content="0"/><meta name="dtb:maxPageNumber" content="0"/></head><docTitle><text>The Price of Slowing Down</text></docTitle><navMap><navPoint id="nav-1" playOrder="1"><navLabel><text>Introduction: The cost of time</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch001.xhtml"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-2" playOrder="2"><navLabel><text>1. Who gets to keep building?</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml"/><navPoint id="nav-3" playOrder="3"><navLabel><text>Defining the slowdown</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-defining-the-slowdown"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-4" playOrder="4"><navLabel><text>Assessment costs can shape market entry</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-assessment-costs-can-shape-market-entry"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-5" playOrder="5"><navLabel><text>Accenture’s commercial and evaluation roles</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-accentures-commercial-and-evaluation-roles"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-6" playOrder="6"><navLabel><text>Who would control access to the market?</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-who-would-control-access-to-the-market"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-7" playOrder="7"><navLabel><text>Coordination and competition law</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-coordination-and-competition-law"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-8" playOrder="8"><navLabel><text>The lead over China and the place of open models</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-the-lead-over-china-and-the-place-of-open-models"/></navPoint><navPoint id="nav-9" playOrder="9"><navLabel><text>The financial interests at stake</text></navLabel><content src="text/ch002.xhtml#c1-the-financial-interests-at-stake"/></navPoint